  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 330 AM EDT Sunday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Cool and wet weather continues today. Gradually \ndrying out tonight.\n\nA frontal boundary is situated well S of the area this morning. A \nCAD airmass is in place with high pressure over New England. \nLocally, it's a moist and (relatively) cool early morning. The \ncoverage of rain has been lower than expected, perhaps owing to \nearlier convective activity well to our S. There is a batch of \nlight- moderate rain currently moving through the Eastern Shore \nwith drizzle or very light rain elsewhere. \n\nGiven observational and modeling trends, the coverage of rain later \ntoday also will probably be less than originally anticipated. A wave \nof low pressure will ride along the frontal boundary later this \nmorning/early afternoon and do expect more widespread rain to \ndevelop across SE VA and especially NE NC during this time. CAMs \nalso highlight another batch potentially moving through the Piedmont \nas well. With the lower coverage and intensity of the rain, QPF has \nalso trended lower and additional totals today will be generally be \nin the 0.10-0.25\" range, except 0.25-0.5\" for far SE VA and NE NC. \nThe coolest temps, mid to upper 60s, will be well inland today (in \nthe core of the CAD airmass), with lower 70s near the coast. Rain \ntapers off by late this afternoon and evening as drier air begins to \nmove in behind another cold front, though low-end PoPs remain in \nplace across the SE through early tonight. Clouds will be slow to \nclear and mostly cloudy skies persist tonight, with low temperatures \nranging from the mid-upper 50s W of I-95 and lower-mid 60s E of I-95.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Seasonable temperatures and dry for the upcoming \nwork week.  \n\nCold advection will initially lag behind the secondary front on \nMonday. So, temps will actually be warmer than this weekend for most \nlocations as the sun makes a reappearance. Highs should be in the \n70s for all of our CWA, warmest W of the Chesapeake Bay. The more \npronounced cooler and drier air arrives Monday evening into Monday \nnight as ~1025 mb high pressure builds down toward the Mid-Atlantic \nfrom the Great Lakes region. Markedly cooler Monday night as lows \ndip into the 50s for SE VA/NE NC, with mid to upper 40s elsewhere. \nThe cooler/dry theme continues into the midweek period as the high \npressure system builds overhead. Tuesday should feel most like fall \nwith highs struggling to reach 70 F. Similarly, Tuesday's overnight \nlows will also be the coolest of the week. The current forecast has \n40s for most of the area, though a light onshore wind near the coast \nlikely keeps these areas in the 50s. Should also note that some \nupper 30s are not out of the realm of possibility, especially for \nrural and typically cooler locations in the Piedmont. \n\nUpper-level heights rebound some by Wednesday and a gradual warming \ntrend is expected through the end of the week. Subsidence and a very \ndry 700-300 mb layer will also keep everyone dry. The next chance of \nrain is not until next Saturday night and Sunday, but the exact \ndetails are fuzzy at this range.",
